  "text_md": "We therefore only need to show that σ intertwines the differential, or equivalently,\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nThis follows from applying τ z to equation (4.20), as well as the fact that r is differencedependent.\n\nRemark 4.6. The commutativity condition of Proposition 4.5 will be called weak commutativity.\n\nFrom Proposition 4.5, we can define a 'meromorphic' tensor product M z b N 0 for a pair of FSF modules of U ℏ p d p O qq . However, the resulting module is not FSF anymore. Nevertheless, there is still a natural generalization of associativity condition as follows. Consider now a triple of FSF modules M ' U r r ℏ s s , N ' V r r ℏ s s and P ' W r r ℏ s s , the tensor producr M z ` w bp N w b P 0 q is naturally identified with the vector space\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere as p M z b N q w b P 0 is identified with\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nThese two are not immediately comparable, but they both receive a map from the vector space\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nvia formal expansion, just as in [FBZ04, Chapter 3]. We claim the following result, which is mostly clear from the definition.\n\nProposition 4.7. For a triple of FSF modules M ' U r r ℏ s s , N ' V r r ℏ s s and P ' W r r ℏ s s , the following statements are true.\n\n- The vector space\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nhas a well-defined DG module structure of U ℏ p d p O qq , whose action is induced by p 1 b ∆ w q ∆ z ` w ' p ∆ z b 1 q ∆ w , and whose differential is given by\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nWe denote this module by M z ` w b N w b P 0 , signifying its independence of order.\n\n- Formal expansion as discussed above gives the following maps of DG modules of U ℏ p d p O qq , both of which are equivalences after appropriate ring extension:\n\n<!-- image -->",
